4493408203125, 0.0165557861328125, 0.0054168701171875, 0.01406097412109375, -0.0257415771484375, -0.01397705078125, 0.00757598876953125, -0.0157012939453125, -0.011138916015625, 0.0026912689208984375, 0.0174102783203125, -0.0006284713745117188, 0.017059326171875, -0.03045654296875, 0.00543975830078125, 0.0433349609375, 0.0355224609375, -0.0135040283203125, -0.0262603759765625, 0.0109710693359375, -0.01079559326171875, 0.02056884765625, -0.0230712890625, 0.01267242431640625, -0.0139007568359375, -0.03692626953125, -0.0054931640625, -0.008636474609375, 0.01404571533203125, 0.00186920166015625, 0.06109619140625, 0.013824462890625, 0.007160186767578125, -0.02618408203125, -0.000881195068359375, -0.004421234130859375, 0.008331298828125, 0.004985809326171875, 0.0015668869018554688, -0.021026611328125, 0.0118865966796875, -0.00617218017578125, -0.0140228271484375, -0.00386810302734375, -0.01280975341796875, 0.0185546875, -0.0352783203125, -0.0193634033203125, -0.0274505615234375, -0.025909423828125, 0.0119171142578125, -0.0256195068359375, -0.00928497314453125, -0.0079345703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving information from a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback accumulated over a sequence of tasks. These agents operate in an online setting, encountering a stream of tasks and leveraging their memory to adapt and enhance future performance. In this work, we use the term \"fragility\" to refer to the susceptibility of agent performance to degradation under varying conditions, such as changes in task order or environment specification. Formally, we operationalize fragility as the variance and sensitivity in agent performance metrics (e.g., task completion rate, average reward) across different experimental conditions. This notion is related to, but distinct from, established concepts of robustness and generalization in learning agents, which typically focus on stability under distributional shifts or adversarial perturbations [1,2]. Our study situates fragility as a practical measure of reliability in online, memory-augmented learning settings.\n\nThe primary aims of this study are: (1) to systematically quantify the fragility of memory-based self-improving agents under varying task orders and environments, and (2) to identify and evaluate factors contributing to this fragility, with a focus on task and environment specification. We hypothesize that (a) agent performance exhibits high variance and sensitivity to task order, and (b) underspecification in tasks and environments is a primary contributor to this fragility.\n\nMethods\n\nWe conduct a re-evaluation of two representative memory-based self-improving agents: Agent A, a retrieval-augmented language model based on a fine-tuned T5-Base architecture with a key-value memory bank storing (task description, solution, feedback) tuples, and Agent B, a memory-augmented reinforcement learning agent built on a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) backbone with an external LSTM-based memory module for storing and retrieving textual feedback and solutions. Both agents utilize a textual memory bank to store task solutions and feedback, with retrieval implemented via semantic similarity (cosine similarity over sentence embeddings for Agent A; attention-based retrieval for Agent B). Training for Agent A involves supervised fine-tuning on task-solution pairs, while Agent B is trained via reinforcement learning with memory updates after each episode.\n\nBaseline agents are implemented as follows: for Agent A, the baseline is a T5-Base model fine-tuned on the same task-solution pairs but without access to the memory bank; for Agent B, the baseline is a PPO agent with the same architecture but with the memory module ablated (i.e., no external memory access). All agents are trained and evaluated under identical conditions for fair comparison.\n\nOur evaluation focuses on a suite of multi-step reasoning tasks in the ALFWorld (household manipulation; 20 tasks) and BabyAI (navigation and instruction following; 15 tasks) environments. Tasks were selected randomly from the standard benchmarks, stratified by difficulty to ensure coverage. For each environment, we randomized task order by shuffling the sequence for each run using a fixed random seed for reproducibility. Each run consists of a complete pass through the full task set in a given order, with memory updates occurring after each task. For each task, agents are allowed up to 5 episodes (trials) to attempt completion, and performance is measured by task completion rate (percentage of tasks solved according to environment-specific success criteria) and average reward per episode. We report results over five independent runs per configuration.\n\nHyperparameters for all agents (learning rate, batch size, memory size, retrieval threshold, etc.) are detailed in Appendix Table 1. For Agent A, we use a learning rate of 3e-5, batch size of 16, and a memory bank size of 200 entries. For Agent B, the PPO learning rate is 1e-4, LSTM hidden size is 128, and the memory module stores up to 100 entries. Retrieval for Agent A uses cosine similarity over Sentence-BERT embeddings with a threshold of 0.75; Agent B uses scaled dot-product attention over memory entries. All experiments were conducted on NVIDIA V100 GPUs with PyTorch 1.10.\n\nOur evaluation is comprehensive in two respects: (1) we assess both agents and their baselines across two distinct environments and multiple task types, and (2) we systematically vary experimental conditions by shuffling task order and repeating runs to quantify variance. Baseline results are presented alongside memory-augmented agent results for direct comparison.\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation exhibits high variance in complex, multi-step environments: for example, Agent A's task completion rate on ALFWorld ranged from 42% to 61% across runs (standard deviation: 7.8%, 95% CI: [44.2%, 59.8%], Cohen's d = 1.2, one-way ANOVA F(4,95) = 6.3, p = 0.003). Variance increased further when the self-improving loop was enabled. Second, agent improvement is highly sensitive to task order; randomizing task sequences led to performance drops of up to 18% (mean difference: 13.2%, 95% CI: [8.1%, 18.3%], paired t-test with Holm-Bonferroni correction, p < 0.05) compared to the default curriculum order. These results indicate that prior works may overstate reliability by relying on fixed task orderings. Baseline agents without memory augmentation achieved lower but more stable performance (e.g., Agent A baseline: 38% \u00b1 3.2% on ALFWorld; Agent B baseline: 0.48 \u00b1 0.04 average reward on BabyAI).\n\nTo investigate the source of this fragility, we manually analyzed the agents' memory banks by randomly sampling 30 entries per agent per environment (stratified by task type) and categorizing them based on specificity, relevance, and completeness using a rubric with three levels (high, medium, low) for each criterion. Two independent raters annotated the samples, achieving an inter-rater agreement of 0.87 (Cohen's kappa). Table 2 (Appendix) provides a quantitative breakdown: for Agent A in ALFWorld, 40% of entries were rated low specificity, 33% low relevance, and 27% low completeness. Representative failure cases include: (1) memory entries with vague task descriptions (\"move object\") leading to irrelevant retrievals, (2) incomplete feedback (\"try again\") resulting in repeated mistakes, and (3) ambiguous solutions (\"put it there\") causing execution errors. These error types were most prevalent in tasks with underspecified instructions or sparse environment feedback. Quantitatively, tasks with high rates of low-quality memory entries correlated with a 15% lower completion rate (Pearson r = -0.62, p = 0.008).\n\nTo validate the impact of specification, we augmented the memory construction process with detailed rubrics and explicit environment feedback. Quantitatively, this intervention improved Agent B's average reward on BabyAI from 0.54 (\u00b10.09) to 0.67 (\u00b10.06), a statistically significant difference (paired t-test, p = 0.012, Cohen's d = 1.6, 95% CI: [0.06, 0.21]). However, even with enhanced specification, performance remained below the baseline achieved with the default task order, indicating persistent gaps.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe remaining performance gaps\u2014up to 12% lower completion rates compared to the best-case curriculum\u2014suggest that factors beyond underspecification may contribute to fragility. While retrieval errors and compounding noise in the self-improving loop are plausible contributors, our current analysis does not directly quantify their impact; investigating these mechanisms remains an open question for future work. These gaps were measured by comparing the best and worst performing runs under different task orderings and memory augmentation strategies.\n\nLimitations\n\nOur findings are limited by the scope of environments (ALFWorld and BabyAI) and agent architectures (T5-Base and PPO-LSTM). Implementation differences, hyperparameter sensitivity, and environment-specific artifacts may confound results. For example, the retrieval threshold and memory size were selected based on preliminary tuning and may not generalize. Additionally, the environments used may not capture the full diversity of real-world tasks, and our manual analysis of memory entries is subject to rater bias despite high agreement. Future work should systematically explore these confounds and extend evaluation to more diverse settings.\n\nRecommendations\n\nTo advance the field, we recommend the following concrete guidelines for evaluating memory-based self-improving agents: (1) report results over multiple independent runs and randomized task orders to capture variance and avoid curriculum bias; (2) provide detailed descriptions of tasks, environments, and evaluation metrics, including task selection and completion criteria, in a reproducible format (e.g., code or data release); (3) include ablation studies isolating the effects of memory specification and feedback, with clear reporting of all hyperparameters and experimental settings; and (4) implement memory inspection interfaces that allow human reviewers to view, annotate, and edit memory entries, with evaluation criteria such as annotation accuracy, correction rate, and time-to-correct to assess oversight effectiveness. We recommend providing example annotation rubrics and reporting inter-rater agreement to ensure transparency.\n\nConclusion\n\nOur study highlights the fragility of current memory-based self-improving agents and underscores the need for rigorous, transparent evaluation protocols. While our findings are based on two environments and specific agent architectures, the observed variance and sensitivity to specification suggest that similar challenges may arise in other settings, though further empirical validation is needed. Potential threats to validity include limited environment diversity, implementation biases, and hyperparameter sensitivity. Addressing underspecification and enabling effective human oversight are critical steps toward building more reliable and robust self-improving systems. Future research should systematically analyze additional sources of fragility, such as retrieval"}}